 **Periodicity and persistence.** We computed a Lomb–Scargle (LS) periodogram from 0.1
-to 60 d for stacks A, B and A+B. We also ran a general-purpose rotation-period search
-(ACF and periodogram based) on the A+B stack, treating it exactly like a SMARTS star.
+to 60 d for stacks A, B and A+B. We also ran the rotation-period pipeline described
+below on the A+B stack, treating it exactly like a SMARTS star.
+
+*The period search.* The pipeline is an in-development rewrite of SpinSpotter (Holcomb
+et al. 2022). It pools candidate periods from three methods (a histogram of
+autocorrelation-peak separations, a Lomb–Scargle periodogram and an FFT-based
+autocorrelation function (ACF), 10 candidates each). It then fits a comb model, peaks at
+integer multiples of the period, to the light curve's ACF for every candidate. A period is
+returned only if the best fit passes quality gates: at least 4 fitted ACF peaks, at least
+80% of them with positive height and inside their fitting windows, a mean peak-height S/N
+of at least 1, and checks against half- and double-period aliases. Otherwise it declines.
+It was developed on synthetic light curves and earlier SMARTS subsets; the 1,000-file
+test sample of Appendix B was not used to tune it. We have not measured its
+false-positive rate on pure red noise.
+
